# Spawning a role on each host

Read this when spawning an isolated subagent or before starting a worker.

| Host | Mechanism | Projected file |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Claude Code | The subagent named `<role>` (Agent tool or `@agent-<role>`) | `.claude/agents/<role>.md` |
| Codex CLI | `collaboration.spawn_agent` with `agent_type: <role>` when available | `.codex/agents/<role>.toml` |
| Antigravity | `invoke_subagent` with the role name | `.agents/agents/<role>.md` |

- Give a projected named role only the payload. Repair a missing or stale projection with `devkit status` and an authorized `devkit sync`; never edit projected files.
- A missing role does not prove the root is untrusted; diagnose availability separately from trust.
- If the named role cannot load and host project-instruction loading is permitted, spawn a generic native subagent that follows the effective managed/override contract by absolute path, and report the emulation. Never substitute the upstream contract for an override.
- An explicit host trust restriction stops this fallback; do not bypass it with another role, tool, path or host setting. Trust changes belong to the user.
- Start a worker only when native spawning, its nested roles and the approval capability its operations need are available; otherwise run the unit in `direct` when allowed, or stop with `needs_input` when isolation is required. Availability does not promise an approval.
- Codex runs both workers `emulated` under `workspace-write`, with no role or path enforcement: their contract's absolute-path and per-operation approval rules are the only boundary.
